  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Phenol is manufactured by heating chlorobenzene with 10% aqueous NaOH solution at about 623K under 300 atm and in the presence of copper salt acting as a catalyst to form sodium phenoxide. The sodium salt when treated with dil. HCl, gives phenol. This method is known as Dow&#39;s process.&nbsp;</p><p>When phenol is heated with&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CHCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;in the presence of aqueous&nbsp;NaOH, an aldehydic group&nbsp;(&minus;CHO)&nbsp;get introduced in the ring at a position ortho to the phenolic group. This reaction is known as the Reimer-Tiemann reaction.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0008_982828c61fc3.png\" style=\"width: 450px; height: 163px;\" /></p>"
  }
